# A panic that crossed a `bracket` with nothing left to catch it ends the
# program -- and ends it the same way on both backends.
#
# bracket.dawn asks the routing half of this claim (does `catch_fault` decline
# a panic that crossed a bracket) and can only ask it with an outer
# `catch_panic` in place, because a corpus program that dies has no further
# output to compare. This file asks the other half: what happens when nothing
# catches it at all. That is a different code path on native -- `dawn_reraise`
# walks the handler chain, finds nothing that takes a panic, and reports out of
# the saved failure buffer rather than out of a message whose frame is gone --
# and the only thing that says it agrees with the JVM is a run that lets it
# happen.
#
# So this program is *supposed* to exit non-zero, which the harness has to be
# told: the marker file `bracket_fatal.exits-nonzero` is that. What is checked
# is the stdout up to the failure (against .expect), and then the stderr and
# the exit code, compared between the backends. A backend that swallowed the
# panic would show up as a 0 against a 1.
use std/io

fn open(name: String) -> String !io = {
  println("  acquire " ++ name)
  name
}

fn close(name: String) -> Unit !io = println("  release " ++ name)

## Named rather than a lambda: `fn(a) => panic(...)` leaves `B` with nothing
## to infer it from.
fn use_panics(a: String) -> Int !io = panic("a panic that crossed a bracket")

pub fn main() -> Unit !io = {
  println("nothing here catches a panic")
  let v = bracket(open("r"), close, use_panics)
  # Unreachable. The release above ran and the panic resumed with no handler
  # left, so the program is already over. Printing is how a backend that got
  # this wrong says so, rather than passing quietly.
  println("WRONG: bracket swallowed it and answered " ++ to_string(v))
}
